    "q-83824": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83824",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
